Credits (from the history file of the C port)
---------------------------------------------
* Zork, called Dungeon at the time, was created at the MIT Laboratory for Computer
  Science (Programming Technology Division) by Tim Anderson, Marc Blank, Bruce
  Daniels and Dave Lebling, in MDL. It was inspired by Adventure (Will Crowther and
  Don Woods) and Dungeons and Dragons (Gary Gygax and Dave Arneson).
* Translated from MDL into FORTRAN IV by "a somewhat paranoid DEC engineer who
  prefers to remain anonymous" (R. M. Supnik is named in the source headers).
* DEC FORTRAN to Unix f77: Randy Dietrich, Lynn Cochran and Sig Peterson (1981).
* PDP to VAX, full save/restore and the debugging package: Bill Randle (1985).
* Cleanup: John Gilmore (1986) and Bill Randle (1987).
* f77 to C (11 March 1991): Ian Lance Taylor, using f2c (David Gay, Stu Feldman,
  Mark Maimone, Norm Schryer). Testing: Andre Srinivasan. MS-DOS port: Jonathan Mark.
* The 1977 MIT source in the Tapes of Tech Square collection (MIT Libraries,
  Department of Distinctive Collections, MIT No Attribution licence) is the
  ancestor of all of this; the maintainers of that collection preserved it.
* This Python translation: machine-translated from the C port by tools/c2py.py and
  checked against the C program by tools/crosscheck.py (see README).

Changes made in this translation
--------------------------------
* The game logic is translated mechanically; its behaviour is meant to be identical
  and was cross-checked move for move against the C program.
* The "!command" shell escape of the C version has been removed (it would let anyone
  who can message the game run commands on the host).
* The GDT debugging tool and the in-game SAVE/RESTORE verbs are not available: the
  host (MeshToys) saves each player's game automatically after every move.
* zork/dtextc.dat (the game's message text and data tables) is the C port's data file,
  unchanged.

